    "narrative_title": "Middle East conflict drives global oil supply shock",
    "narrative_summary": "Escalating Mideast hostilities and the U.S.–Iran standoff are the dominant drivers of a renewed global oil-supply shock: attacks on shipping and pipelines, sanctions and countermeasures, and military/political posturing have tightened effective flows even as some Gulf exports and repairs attempt to restore volumes. Markets, producers (OPEC+), and governments respond with SPR releases, waivers, output guidance and diplomacy, producing fast feedback loops between security events, sanctions/diplomacy, and oil-price volatility with knock-on effects for finance, trade and transport security. Mediators, sanctions actions and infrastructure restorations act as partial stabilizers, while episodic strikes, rhetoric and domestic politics periodically re-tighten the system.",
    "scope": "Inside the system: state and non-state actors (Iran, U.S., Saudi Arabia, UAE, Israel, Houthis, proxies), hydrocarbon infrastructure and chokepoints (Strait of Hormuz, Red Sea, pipelines, terminals, refineries, SPR), market mechanisms (physical crude flows, inventories, OPEC+ policy, futures and bank forecasts), enforcement tools (sanctions, waivers), and processes linking them (attacks and reprisals, mediation/diplomacy, repairs and export recoveries, investor and trader reactions).",
